1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:06,433
Right now I'm in the middle of a market, getting a lesson from a grandmother I've never met.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:09,467
"A price tag? Around here, I'm the price tag!"

3
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:12,700
I only came for a thousand won of bean sprouts. And here we are.

4
00:00:12,767 --> 00:00:14,400
Let's rewind three minutes.

5
00:00:14,967 --> 00:00:18,867
Sunday afternoon. I took my kid by the hand to the neighborhood market.

6
00:00:18,933 --> 00:00:22,167
My wife's errand was simple: a thousand won of bean sprouts.

7
00:00:22,233 --> 00:00:26,133
"Dad, what's the difference between a mart, a market and a department store?"

8
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:27,533
"They all just sell stuff."

9
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:33,000
"Um... a department store is the pricey one, a mart is cheaper, and a market is... the cheapest?"

10
00:00:33,067 --> 00:00:37,267
Of all places, I said that right in front of a vegetable stall.

11
00:00:37,333 --> 00:00:39,533
"Young man, did you just call the market the cheap place?"

12
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:43,067
"People don't come to the market because it's cheap. They come because they trust it!"

13
00:00:43,133 --> 00:00:44,867
One talking-to, earned.

14
00:00:45,433 --> 00:00:51,200
"N-no, ma'am, that's not what I... where's the price tag on these bean sprouts?"

15
00:00:51,267 --> 00:00:56,400
"A price tag? Around here, I'm the price tag! If you want to know, just ask."

16
00:00:56,467 --> 00:00:58,633
She was right, so I had nothing to say.

17
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:00,233
That's talking-to number two.

18
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:03,400
"Dad, if you don't know, just ask the grandma."

19
00:01:03,467 --> 00:01:06,200
"That's right! Smart kid."

20
00:01:06,767 --> 00:01:10,500
I slipped behind the puffed-rice machine and quietly took out my phone.

21
00:01:10,567 --> 00:01:15,567
The creature known as Dad, when cornered, will search the internet anywhere.

22
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:18,667
To be fair, it is confusing. These days they've all blended together.

23
00:01:18,733 --> 00:01:25,100
Department stores have mart-like food halls in the basement, marts have clothing shops inside, and markets take cards now.

24
00:01:25,167 --> 00:01:30,267
Even when I go to the mart, I say I'm "going to the jang." And jang originally means market.

25
00:01:30,333 --> 00:01:34,067
So I decided to forget price and look at just one thing.

26
00:01:34,133 --> 00:01:38,200
Right up until you pay for it, whose was it?

27
00:01:38,267 --> 00:01:40,467
And right then, the puffed-rice machine went off.

28
00:01:41,033 --> 00:01:47,100
First, the market. In late Joseon Korea, markets that opened once every five days spread across the country.

29
00:01:47,167 --> 00:01:48,933
More than a thousand of them, it's said.

30
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,500
On market day, traders gathered with goods on their heads and backs, then moved on to the next market.

31
00:01:54,567 --> 00:02:00,000
The first permanent, open-every-day market is usually said to be Gwangjang Market in Jongno, Seoul, in 1905.

32
00:02:00,567 --> 00:02:03,900
At a market, the goods usually belong to the owner of that stall.

33
00:02:03,967 --> 00:02:08,367
They buy it themselves, sell it themselves, and if it doesn't sell, it's their loss.

34
00:02:08,433 --> 00:02:13,967
That's why the owner can say "here, have one more." Only the owner can give a freebie.

35
00:02:14,033 --> 00:02:16,267
"It's my stuff, so it's up to me!"

36
00:02:16,333 --> 00:02:22,433
That's also why it doesn't need a price tag. The owner is standing right there to answer you.

37
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,167
Next, the department store. In 1852, at a Paris shop called Le Bon Marché,

38
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:32,667
a man named Aristide Boucicaut started doing business in a strange new way.

39
00:02:32,733 --> 00:02:35,900
He put a price tag on every item, so nobody had to haggle.

40
00:02:35,967 --> 00:02:40,567
He let people just look without buying. He even took things back if you didn't like them.

41
00:02:40,633 --> 00:02:45,367
So the price tag is a fairly new product. It only caught on about 170 years ago.

42
00:02:45,433 --> 00:02:50,300
The grandmother was right. Before that, a person was the price tag.

43
00:02:50,867 --> 00:02:55,133
Department stores arrived in Korea in 1930s Seoul.

44
00:02:55,200 --> 00:03:00,467
But most of the clothes and cosmetics sold in a Korean department store don't belong to the store.

45
00:03:00,533 --> 00:03:05,900
Often the brands bring in their own goods and sell them, and the store takes a commission on what sells.

46
00:03:05,967 --> 00:03:10,033
So a department store is closer to a very fancy landlord.

47
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:17,500
Last, the mart. In 1993, Korea's first big discount store opened in Chang-dong, Seoul.

48
00:03:17,567 --> 00:03:23,333
A mart buys huge amounts straight from the makers. From that moment, the goods are the mart's.

49
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:26,867
So the mart sets the price, and the buy-one-get-one deals too.

50
00:03:26,933 --> 00:03:30,767
By the way, "mart" is just an English word for marketplace.

51
00:03:30,833 --> 00:03:36,900
So my kid's question was really: what's the difference between a marketplace, a market and a department store?

52
00:03:37,467 --> 00:03:39,033
So, to sum up:

53
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:40,967
At a market, it's the owner's goods.

54
00:03:41,033 --> 00:03:42,467
At a mart, it's the mart's goods.

55
00:03:42,533 --> 00:03:44,567
At a department store, it's the brands' goods.

56
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,167
I came out from behind the machine and stood next to my kid.

57
00:03:49,233 --> 00:03:54,133
"Remember how Dad sorted them into pricey and cheap? That was wrong."

58
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:57,067
"The real difference is who owns the stuff."

59
00:03:57,133 --> 00:04:04,733
"At a market every stall has its own boss, so everything here is the grandma's. That's why she can give you one extra."

60
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:09,400
"A mart buys a whole lot of stuff and sells it as the mart's own."

61
00:04:09,467 --> 00:04:15,500
"And a department store is lots of companies' shops gathered in one big building. The stuff belongs to each company."

62
00:04:15,567 --> 00:04:19,933
"So it's not that one place is better or worse. They just have different owners."

63
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,967
My kid stared at the grandmother for a while, then walked up and asked:

64
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:26,233
"Grandma, is all of this yours?"

65
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:29,800
"Of course. I went and got all of it myself at dawn."

66
00:04:29,867 --> 00:04:33,033
"Then a thousand won of bean sprouts, please. And a freebie!"

67
00:04:33,100 --> 00:04:34,267
"Well, look at this one."

68
00:04:34,833 --> 00:04:38,300
The grandmother added one more handful of bean sprouts.

69
00:04:38,367 --> 00:04:40,467
A handful that isn't on any price tag.

70
00:04:41,033 --> 00:04:45,700
Next time you go shopping, try guessing with your kid: whose goods are these?

71
00:04:45,767 --> 00:04:51,367
And at the market, don't look for a price tag. Just ask. There's someone there to answer.

72
00:04:51,433 --> 00:04:54,300
"You come back too, Dad. Next time I'm the one asking the questions!"

73
00:04:54,367 --> 00:04:56,767
That last talking-to was my freebie.

74
00:04:57,333 --> 00:05:01,700
"Dad, why do you have hair all over your body? I don't."

75
00:05:01,767 --> 00:05:04,200
"...The bean sprouts are wilting. Let's hurry."

76
00:05:04,267 --> 00:05:05,433
"Dad, wait for me!"

77
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,533
See you next time. I've got some more searching to do before then.
